From 85a1ea18633908e0479c8496f059072162ea987c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 16:02:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] oneshot: add single-kernel multi-stage pipelined RoCE
 collectives

Upstream base: 1fd6a1fbd8dc4884057046d1ebd93d585c9e4bcf

Rationale:
Large sequence-parallel prefill collectives (> 256 KiB..16 MiB+) previously
sliced payloads across multiple sequential Python-level kernel launches with
DEFAULT_BLOCKS = 64 (16 of 20 GB10 SMs). When overlapped with compute on a
side CUDA stream, yielding the GPU between slices allowed main-stream GEMM
waves to claim all 20 SMs and stall subsequent collective slices for
150-375 us, forcing commit 1fd6a1fbd8dc to cap SparknetOneShotAllReduce at a
single slot (<= _slot_bytes) and fall back to PyNCCL (which steals 4-8 SMs).

Replace multi-launch Python slicing across oneshot_allgather,
oneshot_reducescatter, oneshot_exchange, and all_to_all_rows with a single
persistent low-occupancy kernel launch (for s in T.serial(num_stages):) capped
at SPARKNET_ROCE_COLLECTIVE_BLOCKS=8 CTAs (2 of 20 GB10 SMs) and
SPARKNET_ROCE_STAGE_BYTES=4194304 (4 MiB collective bytes per stage). Within
one persistent kernel launch, the 8 resident CTAs alternate across the two
ping-pong RDMA slots (seq = epoch + s, slot = seq & (slots - 1)) and advance
counters[0] to epoch + num_stages at completion without yielding SMs between
stages or allocating additional pinned host memory.

Quality implications:
Bit-exact (zero arithmetic change; identical rank-order reduction and
byte-exact gather/exchange staging).

Tests:
- python3 -m unittest tests/test_oneshot_cpu.py -v (24/24 passed)
- pytest tests/ -v (97 passed, 62 subtests passed)
- 2-boot TP=4 and TP=3 hardware verification and GEMM-overlap benchmarks
  across 48 KiB - 16 MiB on GB10 ConnectX-7 RoCEv2

Upstream status:
Candidate for dgx-spark-networking main.
